$ (containment) takes strings on both sides (diag.dollar_string_only,
warning, on the $ token): a numeric-prefixed operand fires. In
.NOT. "a" $ sList unary .NOT. binds tighter than $, putting a boolean on
the left; diag.parenthesize_negated_comparison reports that cause (error:
.NOT. on a string literal raises) and dollar_string_only defers to it.
The intended test is .NOT. ("a" $ sList).
-- input.ssl --
:PROCEDURE HasCode;
	:PARAMETERS nCode, sList;
	:IF nCode $ sList;
		:RETURN .T.;
	:ENDIF;
	:IF .NOT. "a" $ sList;
		:RETURN .F.;
	:ENDIF;
	:RETURN .NOT. ("b" $ sList);
:ENDPROC;
-- format --
:PROCEDURE HasCode;
	:PARAMETERS nCode, sList;

	:IF nCode $ sList;
		:RETURN .T.;
	:ENDIF;

	:IF .NOT. "a" $ sList;
		:RETURN .F.;
	:ENDIF;

	:RETURN .NOT. ("b" $ sList);
:ENDPROC;
-- diagnostics --
3:12-3:13 warning dollar_string_only: The '$' containment operator only works on strings. Non-string operands cause error
6:6-6:23 error parenthesize_negated_comparison: '.NOT.' binds before '$', so it applies to a string literal and raises at run time - write '.NOT. (a $ b)' to negate the comparison
